import{_ as a,o as t,c as o,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.CDpkVv68.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Introduction: a living world shaped by its people","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/guide/introduction.md","filePath":"en/guide/introduction.md"}'),n={name:"en/guide/introduction.md"};function i(s,e,d,l,c,h){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="introduction-a-living-world-shaped-by-its-people" tabindex="-1">Introduction: a living world shaped by its people <a class="header-anchor" href="#introduction-a-living-world-shaped-by-its-people" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Introduction: a living world shaped by its people&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>Status:</strong> Design overview · Modern Combat and the expanded world, craft and magic systems are Proposed; prototype numbers are Experimental. Accepted foundations retain their stated scope. Described features are not necessarily available to play.</p><h2 id="tl-dr" tabindex="-1">TL;DR <a class="header-anchor" href="#tl-dr" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;TL;DR&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Syrolands explores a living sandbox where the world, your knowledge and your choices matter.</li><li>Combat should be easy to operate and hard to master, with Modern and Legacy options.</li><li>Crafts, exploration and magic offer identities beyond a single combat power ladder.</li></ul><h2 id="a-world-that-can-change-your-plans" tabindex="-1">A world that can change your plans <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-world-that-can-change-your-plans" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A world that can change your plans&quot;">​</a></h2><p>You set out along a familiar road, but a fallen tree blocks the narrow path. Going around takes time; clearing it needs tools; the obstruction may be useful to someone defending the crossing. This is the proposed living-world direction: resources, terrain and objects can physically affect routes and conflict.</p><p>Syrolands is a sandbox laboratory inspired by the freedom of Ultima Online. You do not need to know that game to follow the idea: build a character through what you learn and do, then find a place in a world shared with other people. Environmental change needs boundaries and recovery rules; an interactive world is not a promise that everything can be destroyed.</p><h2 id="combat-with-room-to-grow" tabindex="-1">Combat with room to grow <a class="header-anchor" href="#combat-with-room-to-grow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Combat with room to grow&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Modern Combat is the preferred experimental direction: select a target and initiate an available action when you choose to commit. Legacy Auto-Attack remains a supported design alternative with automatic cadence. Both should be approachable and share fair, readable rules; neither mode is promised as newly playable here.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>What you read</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Spacing and reach</td><td>A spear&#39;s advantage at distance can become awkward up close</td></tr><tr><td>Timing and stamina</td><td>A heavy commitment creates pressure, but a miss leaves recovery to punish</td></tr><tr><td>Special Attacks and Flow</td><td>Learned options and a sustainable rhythm reward judgment rather than button mashing</td></tr><tr><td>Local trauma and equipment</td><td>The condition of a limb or a piece of armor can change the problem you face</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The aim is easy operation with depth in decisions. Player execution and character expertise coexist; information helps you choose without guaranteeing a hit.</p><h2 id="knowledge-is-useful-in-the-moment" tabindex="-1">Knowledge is useful in the moment <a class="header-anchor" href="#knowledge-is-useful-in-the-moment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Knowledge is useful in the moment&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Anatomy, Arms Lore, Animal Lore and Tracking point toward different kinds of understanding: bodies, equipment, creatures and traces. Skills should give useful information and capability. Recognizing a problem does not solve it automatically, and knowing more should not mean seeing every hidden number.</p><h2 id="crafts-belong-to-adventurers" tabindex="-1">Crafts belong to adventurers <a class="header-anchor" href="#crafts-belong-to-adventurers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Crafts belong to adventurers&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Professions should offer field, combat-support and economic utility, without requiring an isolated “crafter alt” lifestyle. A cook helps an expedition prepare; an alchemist brings useful mixtures; a smith or tailor understands damaged equipment. Carpentry and Tinkering can support structures, tools and mechanisms.</p><p>Crafting is proposed as interactive work shaped by materials and execution. Techniques, experimentation, reputation and knowledge matter alongside skill. Being known for dependable field gear or remarkable food can be a meaningful identity.</p><h2 id="discover-your-specialty" tabindex="-1">Discover your specialty <a class="header-anchor" href="#discover-your-specialty" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Discover your specialty&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Trainers, academies, books, cultures, ruins, quests and exploration can teach techniques or spells. <strong>Skill ≠ Technique ≠ Familiarity ≠ Mastery</strong>: capability, learned options, practiced fluency and deeper expertise are related but distinct. Reaching a skill maximum should not reveal everything. Two experts can become known for very different niches.</p><h2 id="magic-is-learned-and-prepared" tabindex="-1">Magic is learned and prepared <a class="header-anchor" href="#magic-is-learned-and-prepared" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Magic is learned and prepared&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A spell begins with a learned <strong>Core Pattern</strong>. Magery supports execution, Familiarity reflects practice, and Active Memory selects ready patterns. Knowing a spell is different from having it prepared; readiness still does not mean instant casting.</p><p>Spellbooks, scrolls and reagents have proposed tactical and economic roles. Inscription preserves and modifies magical knowledge through notation, variants and physical works. Capacity, reference delays and many costs remain open questions.</p><h2 id="items-with-a-story" tabindex="-1">Items with a story <a class="header-anchor" href="#items-with-a-story" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Items with a story&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Quality means more than raw damage: handling, consistency, integrity, retained performance and meaningful traits should matter. A Masterwork is a rare achievement, not an automatic magical item. Latent properties could make some objects memorable through use and discovery, while ordinary good equipment stays useful and valuable gear remains at risk.</p><h2 id="more-than-one-endgame" tabindex="-1">More than one endgame <a class="header-anchor" href="#more-than-one-endgame" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;More than one endgame&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>A possible ambition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Martial</td><td>Become a respected duelist or dependable hunting companion</td></tr><tr><td>Craft</td><td>Develop a recognizable specialty and a reputation for workmanship</td></tr><tr><td>Knowledge and exploration</td><td>Discover techniques, materials, places or magical traditions</td></tr><tr><td>Economy</td><td>Build trusted trade relationships and supply useful goods</td></tr><tr><td>Social and political</td><td>Organize a community or influence shared projects</td></tr><tr><td>Reputation</td><td>Become known for what you contribute, not only your skill totals</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are design aspirations, not a list of delivered systems. Start with the <a href="/Syrolands-Public/en/guide/">Guide</a> to explore one path, or read the <a href="/Syrolands-Public/en/game-design/vision.html">project vision</a> for the detailed design context.</p>',26)])])}const m=a(n,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
